-cached; decoding, hashing and parsing do not repeat each rendered frame. Temporary
-decoded JSON is dropped instead of being retained beside the parsed geometry.
-Plain legacy JSON remains supported. The shared loader uses the workspace's
-existing base64, flate2 and sha2 versions, bounds decoded documents to 16 MiB,
-checks complete gzip boundaries, validates SHA-256 and rejects malformed input.
+The 655 models have 82,988,790 decoded JSON bytes. Their binary gzip payloads total
+12,307,304 bytes and identity records total 165,604 bytes: 12,472,908 embedded
+bytes, compared with 19,809,542 bytes of previous envelopes/plain documents.
+The largest payload is the shared human geometry, at 1,312,183 gzip bytes. Keeping
+gzip avoids adding roughly 63 MB of decoded JSON to the executable or WebAssembly
+data segment. This storage count is not a measured executable size or startup
+benchmark; compiler/linker and web-transfer compression also affect those sizes.
 
-The shared human geometry envelope is the only compressed document large enough
-to need bounded transport chunks. Its canonical `.json` path contains a small
-ordered descriptor; text chunks are at most 350,000 bytes. A generated sibling
-`.include.rs` uses `concat!(include_str!(...))` to provide its complete envelope
-at compile time. It has no runtime file-system dependency. No LFS, credentials,
-binary transport or duplicated full canonical model file is needed.
+Each catalog still decodes inside its existing `OnceLock` initializer. Parsed
+meshes/rigs are cached; decoding and parsing do not run every frame. Temporary JSON
+is dropped afterward. Human rigs still refer to one shared geometry library.
+The include macro embeds metadata with `include_str!` and binary gzip with
+`include_bytes!`; there is no runtime filesystem or network dependency.
 
